    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the purchase of coverage under family health plus by voluntary employee benefit associations",
    "basePrintNo": "S6024",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6024-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §369-ff, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6024\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to the purchase\nof coverage under family health plus by voluntary employee benefit\nassociations\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill authorizes voluntary employee benefit associations (VEBAs)\nthroughout the State to \"buy in\" to Family Health Plus, allowing them\nto purchase Family Health Plus coverage for their members regardless\nof income eligibility.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Social Services Law (SSL) § 369-ff to\npermit voluntary employee benefit associations (VEBAs) to \"buy in\" to\nFamily Health Plus, regardless of income eligibility.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides that the bill would take effect\nimmediately.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nSSL § 369-ee established the Family Health Plus program to provide\ncomprehensive health care insurance for uninsured persons who have too\nmuch income or resources to qualify for Medicaid.\n\nSSL § 369-ff authorizes employers and Taft-Hartley funds to \"buy in\"\nto Family Health Plus. Employers and Taft-Hartley funds are permitted\nto offer Family Health Plus to all employees or members, regardless of\nincome eligibility. The employer/Taft-Hartley fund is responsible for\npayment of at least 70 percent of the premium, and the employee or\nmember is responsible for the balance of the premium. For those\nemployees or members who are eligible for Family Health Plus, Child\nHealth Plus or Medicaid, the State will pay for the employee's share\nof the premium through the Premium Assistance Program. The\nCommissioner of Health is authorized to subsidize the employer's or\nTaft-Hartley fund's share of the premium under specific conditions.\n\nSSL § 369-ff also: (1) exempts individuals enrolled in the program in\nthis manner from crowd-out provisions; (2) makes plans offered in this\nway community rated; (3) allows the Commissioner of Health\n(Commissioner) to require Family Health Plus plans to cover all\nbenefits provided for in Child Health Plus for children under the age\nof 21; (4) directs employers/Taft-Hartley funds who choose to\nparticipate in the plan to provide information to all employees or\nmembers about employer sponsored health insurance or premium\nassistance; and (5) permits the Commissioner or the Commissioner's\ndesignee to act as a health plan coordinator to facilitate the\noffering of multiple health plans.\n\nPublic Health Law § 2511 (18) establishes a Premium Assistance Program\nto assist beneficiaries with the purchase of coverage that includes\ncoverage of a child under Child Health Plus, Family Health Plus and\nMedicaid.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nIn 2007, the State enacted SSL § 369-ff to permit employers and\nTaft-Hartley funds that offer health coverage to \"buy-in\" to Family\nHealth Plus, allowing workers to participate in employment-based\nhealth coverage. This bill extends the ability to \"buy-in\" to Family\nHealth Plus to a voluntary employee benefit association (VEBA)\nestablished in accordance with the requirements of § 501(c)(9) of the\nfederal Internal Revenue Code.\n\nAuthorizing VEBAs to \"buy-in\" to Family Health Plus permits their\nmembers to receive traditional public health coverage, with the VEBA\nand the employee sharing the costs. This will make group health\ninsurance coverage accessible to individuals who are currently\nuninsured, and in many instances may make it more affordable.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill will not have any fiscal impact to the State in fiscal year\n2009-10.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe bill would be effective immediately.\n",
